{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DESIGN SPIKE — failure-disposition contract (plan-review), NEW advisory criterion `project.failure-disposition-contract`\n\nDesign-only. No criterion authored/registered, no code landed, no billable LLM gate run. Trigger prototyped as local Python heuristics and back-tested against the whole store (3007 tickets, exported via `rebar.export_tickets` NDJSON); positives/negatives hand-labeled.\n\n## TL;DR RECOMMENDATIONS\n1. **Trigger = HYBRID**: a generous DETERMINISTIC pre-gate (high recall) + the LLM overlay GATE as the applicability authority — the exact `removal-rationale`/T5b shape. DET keys on failure-semantics VOCABULARY in plan text OR in file_impact path/reason — NOT on touching error-handling code. This is what fires on the `8fbd` CONFIG flip.\n2. **PASS predicate** = per affected failure path, an explicit disposition word (retryable/transient vs fatal/permanent); a labeled \"Failure-disposition contract:\" section is coached/recommended, not strictly required. Clause (2) (fallback-preservation) is CONDITIONAL — applies only when the plan adds/alters a secondary/fallback arm.\n3. **Granularity** = one statement per FAILURE-HANDLING ARM the plan adds/alters (not per exception subclass, not one blanket line).\n4. **Tier = ADVISORY only.** A deterministic BLOCK-floor is NOT warranted (evidence: the cleanest possible floor still hard-blocks ~26/32 false positives). Any future promotion goes through the standard dogfood-gated advisory→blocking path.\n5. **exec = 1-TURN** (plan-text reasoning, no codebase grounding); scope = container+leaf; **do NOT set `suppress_types:[\"bug\"]`** (the origin 8fbd is a bug — suppressing bugs would miss the motivating incident).\n\n## 1. TRIGGER MECHANISM + THE CONFIG-ONLY CASE (make-or-break)\nHouse precedent: `det_gate_rules.py` — `removal-rationale`/`asserted-capability` are DET-PRE-GATED then LLM-routed. Docstring: *\"these gates are conservative PRE-FILTERS, not applicability judges — a plan skips a criterion only on TOTAL subject-vocabulary absence; a fired plan still routes through the unchanged LLM router, which remains the applicability authority.\"* Project criteria carry their own DET trigger as a `trigger` list in `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` (consumed by `project_trigger_fires`; arms = `text_all` / `file_impact_globs` / `file_impact_reason_re`).\n\n**Recommended DET trigger** (one rule; OR across arms):\n- `text_all` (single disjunction regex): `fallback | fall back | failover | retry | backoff | jitter | timeout | deadline | circuit-breaker | disposition | retryable | non-retryable | transient | error_code | exception type | graceful degrad* | degraded-mode | INDETERMINATE | WAIT_AND_RETRY | NEEDS_INVESTIGATION | exit-11 | fail-(open|closed|fast|safe) | swallow* | re-raise | \"surfaces X as\" | should_fall_back | classify_llm_failure | masks (retry|error|failure|throttle)`\n- `file_impact_reason_re` (SAME vocab over each file_impact {path,reason}): `fallback|failover|retry|timeout|circuit|disposition|fail-(open|closed)|error[_ ]?code`\n\n**Why this solves the config-only origin (`8fbd-e9d5`, a bug, touches rebar.toml only):**\n- Its plan text says \"fallback\"/\"fallback chain\"/\"fallback arm\"/\"resilience setting\" repeatedly.\n- Its file_impact carries `{path: rebar.toml, reason: \"Configure direct-Anthropic fallbacks\"}`.\n- → BOTH the text arm and the file_impact_reason arm fire. **VERIFIED: `8fbd-e9d5` FIRES.** The trigger keys on failure-semantics VOCABULARY (incl. the declared file-impact reason), so a thin .toml flip is caught even though it touches zero error-handling *code*. That is the whole point: the code-review overlay (c180) covers code changes; only this plan-level vocabulary/file-impact trigger fires on a pure config flip.\n\nDET alone is INSUFFICIENT for precision (quantified below), so the LLM overlay GATE (a 1-TURN applicability judge, first checklist sub-answer) decides not-applicable→PASS. No LLM is used to TRIGGER; the LLM only judges applicability + pass/fail on a fired plan.\n\n## 2. PASS PREDICATE (objectivity)\nTwo-sub-answer GATE, mirroring `removal-rationale`:\n- **(GATE) `affects_failure_disposition` {yes|no|insufficient}**: does the plan ADD or ALTER a failure/timeout/exception/retry/fallback/circuit-breaker path? `no` → not-applicable → PASS.\n- **(REQ) `disposition_contract_stated` {yes|no|insufficient}**: only when gated in —\n  1. for EACH affected failure path, the plan states the surfaced disposition using an explicit disposition word (retryable/transient vs fatal/permanent); AND\n  2. *[CONDITIONAL — applies ONLY when the plan adds/alters a SECONDARY/fallback arm]* the plan states which disposition wins when the fallback itself fails, and that it preserves the most-recoverable leg (a retryable primary is never masked by the fallback's non-retryable failure — the 1c0d root cause).\n\nA labeled \"Failure-disposition contract:\" section is RECOMMENDED (the coaching move), but an inline statement PASSES if it unambiguously carries a disposition word per affected arm. Inter-rater objectivity comes from the disposition-vocabulary requirement, not prose style. **Clause (2) is a conditional sub-requirement** — not-applicable for a single affected path with no fallback composition.\n\n## 3. GRANULARITY of \"each affected failure path\"\nOne statement per FAILURE-HANDLING ARM the plan adds or alters (the fallback arm, the retry policy, the exception-mapping site) — NOT per exception subclass (enumeration noise) and NOT one blanket line (vacuous green). When ≥2 arms compose (primary+fallback) that composition is itself one \"path\" requiring the clause-(2) winner statement. Reuse T5c's \"one finding per (surface, defect)\" dedup so multiple angles on one arm fold into a single finding.\n\n## 4. TIER — advisory; NO deterministic block-floor\nAdvisory is decided. I additionally tested a narrow DET BLOCK-floor for \"adds a fallback arm but states no disposition at all\":\n- \"adds-a-fallback-arm\" candidates in history: **55**\n- …of which state NO disposition language (the floor set): **32**\n- hand-labeled: only ~4–6 genuine (8fbd, 2876, 79d6, d458 …); the other **~26 are FALSE POSITIVES** where \"fallback\" is a default/config contract — \"uuid fallback\", \"Unavailable fallback contract\", \"DEAD Jira-resolution fallback\", \"run_sweep fallback arm unasserted\", \"single-child fallback\", \"fallback-endpoint test\".\n- → Even the cleanest DET block-floor would HARD-BLOCK ~26 false positives across this store.\n\n**RECOMMENDATION: no block-floor.** Keep the entire criterion advisory (both clauses). Promotion to blocking should later follow the standard dogfood-gated advisory→blocking path in `docs/plan-review-gate.md` (auto-monitored by `criterion_effectiveness.py`), gated on field FP data — never a DET floor. Note: the prevention for an incident CAUSED BY gate LLM fan-out should minimize added gate load — 1-TURN + DET pre-gate (skips ~55% of plans) + advisory does exactly that.\n\n## 5. NECESSITY BACK-TEST (R4) + FP/FN EVIDENCE\n**METHOD:** exported the whole store (3007 tickets) to NDJSON. Plan text = title+description; also scanned file_impact path+reason. Candidate DET trigger run over all tickets; two random labeled samples hand-read + a seeded positive cluster.\n\n**FIRE RATE (recommended trigger):** 45.3% of all 3007 / 41.4% of story+task / 41.9% of non-bug. For calibration, existing DET pre-gates fire far MORE: removal-rationale 86.5%, asserted-capability 96.5%, T13 84.0%, T14 61.6% (over 743 reviewed non-bug tickets). So this trigger is MORE selective than any shipped one — it eliminates ~55% of plans (zero failure-vocabulary).\n\n**PRECISION (Sample A = 40 random FIRED tickets, hand-labeled):** 4 strong TP (5f96 misclassified-degrade, 8303 refusal-as-content, 28a6 fail-closed-on-unreadable-config, 9011 gate-degrades-to-advisory-on-classification-error) + 4 borderline TP (790c, 6af7, d04c, b0f5) + 32 FP → **DET precision ≈ 10% (strict) to 20% (incl. borderline).** FP drivers: rebar's own domain saturates the vocabulary in NON-failure senses (\"uuid fallback\", \"single-child fallback\", \"fallback-endpoint test\", \"transient de-risk\", \"retry/timeout\" being ported/migrated not changed, doc-only mentions).\n\n**FALSE NEGATIVES (Sample B = 40 random NON-fired tickets, hand-labeled):** **0 clear FN** (rule-of-three 95% upper bound ≈ 7.5%). Borderline error-path bugs (8d68 \"returns silently\", 1598 \"raises on every call\") correctly do NOT fire — they fix a broken call, they don't add/alter a disposition CONTRACT. **RECALL on the seeded positive cluster = 11/11 = 100%**: incident-1c0d children (254e backoff/jitter, c180, d209, 9097) + 1c0d itself + origin 8fbd-e9d5 + 5 failure-disposition bugs (5f96/8303/28a6/9011). A residual FN tail of the NARROW base (fail-open/closed/swallow phrasing: 0e1d, 1241, 0d24, 0fa4, 117b, 0dc5) is why the recommended trigger WIDENS to include fail-(open|closed|fast|safe)/swallow/re-raise/surfaces-as (recovers 5/6; 0d24 \"fails silently…degrades\" stays edge-missed — an LLM couldn't see it from that text either).\n\n**INTERPRETATION (honest residual):** a DET trigger cannot separate cleanly — widening to catch the fail-open/closed tail pulls in a large FP tail; the domain floods the vocabulary. This is EXACTLY why the house pattern makes DET a high-recall PRE-FILTER and the LLM overlay the applicability authority. Recommended split: DET pre-filter tuned for FN≈0 (≈45% fire), LLM 1-TURN GATE supplies precision. Residual after the LLM GATE is the LLM's applicability error, monitored post-hoc by `criterion_effectiveness.py` like every advisory criterion.\n\n**R4 NECESSITY — does it earn its place vs CI oracle d209 / code-review c180 / T5b?** YES, non-redundant. It is the ONLY lane that fires on the actual origin — a CONFIG flip (8fbd):\n- d209 (CI regression oracle) catches the class only if someone writes the test; not a plan-time guard.\n- c180 (code-review disposition-masking) covers code diffs, NOT a pure .toml flip.\n- T5b asks whether error-handling EXISTS (`suppress_types:[\"bug\"]`, `scope:leaf`) — it would not even RUN on 8fbd (a bug), and it checks existence, not disposition. `removal-rationale` also suppresses bugs and needs a REMOVAL. **So on 8fbd, neither existing plan-review criterion fires; this one does.** That is the concrete R4 justification.\n\n**PAIRED EVAL CASES (must-fire-and-FAIL / must-not-fire-or-PASS) — pins the boundary:**\n- **TP-1 (config origin, make-or-break):** \"Enable the direct-Anthropic fallback arm in rebar.toml\" (file_impact rebar.toml reason \"configure fallbacks\"), states NO disposition → FIRE + FAIL: \"state, when the fallback arm is unavailable here and when the primary throttles, which disposition surfaces.\"\n- **TN-1 (paired with TP-1):** SAME config flip + \"Failure-disposition contract: a Bedrock 429 stays retryable (exit-11); if the Anthropic fallback's own auth fails the chain preserves the primary's retryable disposition (most-recoverable wins), never surfacing the fallback's non-retryable TypeError.\" → PASS.\n- **TP-2 (clause 1, single path):** \"Map unmatched TypeError in classify_llm_failure to NEEDS_INVESTIGATION\" with no statement of the resulting disposition → FIRE + FAIL (clause 1).\n- **TP-3 (clause 2, incident core):** \"Add an anthropic fallback; on fallback failure surface the fallback's error to the caller\" → FIRE + FAIL (masks a retryable primary; clause 2).\n- **TN-2 (non-failure \"fallback\"):** \"child bin-packing … preserve single-child fallback\" (default-path sense) → DET fires, LLM GATE → not-applicable → PASS. Pins the #1 FP class.\n- **TN-3 (doc/test-only):** \"Docs: … document the fallback contract\" → not-applicable → PASS.\n- **TN-4 (orthogonality with T5b):** a plan adding a new external call WITH retry/backoff/timeout AND an explicit per-path disposition statement → PASS here (contract stated) even though it is a new failure point — guarantees no double-flag with T5b.\n\n## AUTHORING SURFACE (for the implementer; not done here)\n- Register `project.failure-disposition-contract` under `plan_review` in `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` with `default_posture:\"advisory\"`, `exec:\"1-TURN\"`, `scope:[\"container\",\"leaf\"]`, NO `suppress_types`, and the `trigger` list above; add to the `activate.…:[\"plan_review\"]` map.\n- Reviewer overlay: `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_<id>.md` (front-matter + rubric prose, same shape as `plan_review_removal_rationale.md` / `plan_review_T5b.md`), with the two GATE sub-answers and an explicit \"avoid double-flag with T5b — cite the disposition-contract gap specifically\" clause.\n- Eval spec: `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/plan-review-<id>.eval.yaml` seeded with the 7 paired cases above (deterministic `no_fire_on_good_cases` + `recall_on_seeded_defects` scorers, like `plan-review-T10.eval.yaml`).\n- Docs + `rebar explain <id>` per `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md`.\n\n## OPERATOR ESCALATION — behavior-changing decisions\n- **[ITEM 1 — behavior change]** Activating a NEW plan-review criterion adds a 1-TURN LLM sub-call to ~41–45% of plan reviews (those that fire DET). It is advisory (never blocks a claim), but it adds paid-API cost + latency to the plan-review gate — the SAME gate whose LLM fan-out caused incident 1c0d. The 1-TURN + DET-pre-gate (skips ~55%) + advisory choices are deliberately the minimal-load configuration; still, activation is an operator go/no-go.\n- **[NOT suppressing bug type]** Diverges from T5b/removal-rationale (both suppress bugs). Required, or the criterion misses its own origin (8fbd is a bug). Flagged as an explicit, intentional divergence.\n- **[ITEM 4 — block-floor]** I recommend NO deterministic block-floor (evidence: ~26/32 FP hard-blocks). Any later move to blocking (either clause) is behavior-changing and MUST go through the dogfood-gated advisory→blocking promotion path — escalate before flipping; do not add a DET floor."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788135617299078183,"uuid":"3963ca8e-d280-4ca1-af9a-ef2886fd908d"}